    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What this covers</h2>
      <p>Most learning-platform material stops at the application. OpenEduOps starts where that
        material stops: the server, the web stack, the database, the certificate, the firewall, the
        backup, the scheduled task, and the check that tells you when something has changed.</p>
      <p>Two platforms are covered. The <strong>Moodle Foundation Series</strong> builds one Moodle 5.2
        environment on Ubuntu 24.04 LTS from installation through securing, backing up, optimizing, and
        updating it. The <strong>Open edX Foundation</strong> is in progress and covers preparing a
        verified Ubuntu host, installing Open edX Verawood with Tutor, and publishing a first course
        that a separate learner can use.</p>

      <h2>The method</h2>
      <p>Every tutorial follows the same four moves, in the same order:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Make the change.</strong> The actual steps and relevant settings, shown clearly.</li>
        <li><strong>Explain why it matters.</strong> What the change affects, and what it costs.</li>
        <li><strong>Verify the result.</strong> Successful execution is not proof that something works.</li>
        <li><strong>Identify the boundary.</strong> The recovery path, and where the guidance stops.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>When a tutorial uses reusable commands, its companion guide is published as a GitHub Gist,
        so those commands stay copyable and correctable without being re-typed from video.</p>

      <h2>Operational boundaries</h2>
      <p>A working lab, a public demonstration system, and a production environment are not equivalent,
        and the tutorials say so where it matters. A single-server build that is correct for learning
        may be wrong for a service other people depend on: capacity, data protection, change control,
        monitoring obligations, and recovery targets all change the answer.</p>
      <p class="scope-note">Treat the tutorials as practical lab and demonstration guidance. Before
        applying any of it to a system in real use, check it against your own capacity, data
        protection, change-control, and recovery requirements.</p>

      <h2>Scope</h2>
      <p>This site is a discovery layer for published work. It indexes the long-form tutorials and
        links to available companion guides. It does not host courses, sell services, run accounts, or
        collect email addresses. Unreleased videos are not listed here.</p>

      <h2>Contact and project surfaces</h2>
      <p>There is no contact form. The public surfaces for the project are:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a class="ext" href="{{ site.external.youtube }}" rel="noopener">YouTube</a> — where the
          tutorials are published, and where comments are read.</li>
        <li><a class="ext" href="{{ site.external.github }}" rel="noopener">GitHub</a> — where the
          companion command guides and this site are published.</li>
        <li><a class="ext" href="{{ site.external.x }}" rel="noopener">X</a> — short project updates.</li>
      </ul>
